{\small {\sf Abstract. The Kontsevich conjecture states that the moduli space of stable maps from rational curves to a threefold $X$ can be identified with an algebraic space $S(X)$ constructed by a complex of sheaves on $X$ associated to its tangent bundle. We say that $X$ is a Calabi-Yau 3-fold if this complex is of amplitude [[1,1][1,1]]. An enhancement to the Kontsevich conjecture predicts that under some mild assumptions a similar geometric theory of stable maps arises for higher-dimensional projective varieties. This led Oguiso and Pandharipande to introduce a notion of type one generalized Calabi-Yaus, namely those smooth projective $X$ which admit a complex of sheaves of amplitude [[1,p][1,p]] over the tangent bundle and whose moduli space of stable maps is an algebraic space. The existence of such quasi-relative perfect obstruction theories over the cotangent bundle of $X$ is well known in the case of projective hypersurfaces where $X$ is a surface and $p=1$. Our aim is to give an algebraic proof of this result using a new formulation of perfect obstruction theories, and then apply this theorem when $X=Spec R$ for a local Noetherian domain (or more generally an O-ring of a Dedekind domain). As an application we characterize when $R$ satisfies the condition $\alpha_2(R)=0$.}

\normalsize
